<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Fiji</h1><p class="st-meta">1874 CE – 1970 CE · capital Levuka; later Suva</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A British colony for nearly a century that protected Fijian land and chiefly rule while bringing Indian labour for sugar, shaping modern Fiji's divided politics.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji's</a> chiefs ceded the islands to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> on 10 October 1874, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-fiji">Kingdom of Fiji</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a> epidemic in 1875 killed perhaps a third of the Fijian population. The first governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-hamilton-gordon-1st-baron-stanmore">Sir Arthur Gordon</a>, laid down the colony's lasting system, and the capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levuka">Levuka</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suva">Suva</a> in 1882.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-hamilton-gordon-1st-baron-stanmore">Gordon</a> ruled indirectly through chiefs and a Great Council of Chiefs, and forbade the further sale of Fijian land, most of which remains communally owned. To work <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations, run largely by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-sugar-refining-company">Colonial Sugar Refining Company</a>, about 60,000 indentured labourers came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between 1879 and 1916. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratu-sir-lala-sukuna">Ratu Sir Lala Sukuna</a> was the leading Fijian statesman of the colonial era, and Fijian soldiers fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Islands during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> became independent on 10 October 1970, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratu-sir-kamisese-mara">Ratu Sir Kamisese Mara</a> as prime minister. Tensions between indigenous Fijians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-fijians">Indo-Fijians</a>, rooted in colonial policy, contributed to coups in 1987 and later.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-fiji.jpg" alt="Colony of Fiji" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Simitukidia and Lokal Profil · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Fiji_(1924%E2%80%931970).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1874 CE – 1970 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Levuka; later Suva</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arthur Gordon, Lala Sukuna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Methodism, Hinduism, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Fijian, English, Fiji Hindi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/fiji">Fiji</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Fiji</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1922&amp;focus=colony-of-fiji" aria-label="Colony of Fiji on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9535 2834 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Fiji lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9855 3090L9855 3090M9855 3090L4997 651M9855 3090L4997 651M9855 3090L4997 651"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9855 3090h0M4997 651h0M4997 651h0M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9855 3090h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9855 3090h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Levuka. <a href="/atlas?year=1922&amp;focus=colony-of-fiji">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
